---
# Every behaviour ends in a closed turn with nothing left to do: rest,
# not a deadlock.
deadlock_detection: false
---

# The engine's native ask (or secret) runs alongside another call from
# the same reply: the model asks the person a question and, in the same
# reply, starts run_js or bash. Recipe: go/tests/model/README.md.
#
# ask_answer.fizz assumes a question never overlaps other calls, and that
# only the ask's own end resolves it. On the engine calls are parallel,
# so a sibling's end, a stray error line and a provider error all land
# while the Asker (plugins/ask/native.go) still blocks on the person.
#
# One question lives in four places here:
#   asker  the child's Asker: the ask call blocked on the person.
#          none | pending (the call started, the ask not yet put in) |
#          open | job (adopted as a job by providerError) | answered |
#          timedout | cancelled
#   armed  serve's s.asks[id] (supervisor.go emitLocked): gates /answer
#          (ErrNoAsk -> 409 when empty) and /prompt (409 while set).
#   hl     the headless child's hlAsk (plugins/ui/headless.go): whether
#          the next stdin line is the answer or a steer.
#   ask    the row's ask (status.go StatusOf over history): what the page
#          shows, and what makes the status needs-you.
#
# THIS SPEC IS THE INTENDED BEHAVIOUR. Where the product (at 5471918e)
# differs, the difference is a finding, and the spec mutation that
# models the product's behaviour is named next to the action it changes:
#   B1 run_js's end (a recorded "result") clears all three:
#      supervisor.go emitLocked deletes s.asks on every "result",
#      status.go StatusOf drops pending on every "result",
#      headless.go askEnded treats every result as the ask's end.
#   B2 a failing run_js also emits a live-only "error" first
#      (project.go run_js end): serve and hlAsk clear on it too. History
#      then records the result, so the screen clears as in B1.
#   B3 a plain stderr line (supervisor.go pumpStderr -> "error") clears
#      serve's arm only: the page keeps needs-you and its buttons 409.
#      A recorded error note (a hook blocking a steer, a refusal note)
#      clears serve's arm and hlAsk; history keeps the ask, so again
#      needs-you with buttons that 409.
#   B4 a provider error after the sibling's end (the harness re-asks the
#      model with the sibling's result) runs providerError
#      (unreal/session/actor.go): it adopts every foreground call,
#      Blocking or not, as a job and closes the turn with an error; the
#      error and the done clear every arm, and the Asker keeps blocking
#      with no route for an answer until its 10-minute timeout.
# A bash sibling's end is a "call" with tool bash: nothing disarms on
# it, in the product or here.
#
# Fields beyond the four above:
#   status   the row: running | needs-you | done | error | stopped
#   sibling  the other call: none | running | ok | failed | cancelled
#   tool     the sibling's tool: "" | js | bash
#   perr     a provider error was recorded while the ask was open
#   sent     the person sent a message (at most one: bounds the graph)
#   steeredPast  sticky: a message went to the child as a steer or a new
#                turn while the Asker still blocked
#   msgAnswered  sticky: a message was taken as the answer
#   lost     sticky: the ask timed out while it could not be answered
#            from the screen (the 10-minute stall)
# The child stays alive throughout (its death is ask_answer's Exit).
# A secret is the same call with tool "secret": every clear keys on
# tool ask|secret alike, so it is not a separate branch here.

role Session:
    # A turn is running; the model has not replied yet.
    action Init:
        self.status = "running"
        self.asker = "none"
        self.armed = False
        self.hl = False
        self.ask = False
        self.sibling = "none"
        self.tool = ""
        self.perr = False
        self.sent = False
        self.steeredPast = False
        self.msgAnswered = False
        self.lost = False

    # Model: one reply with an ask and a run_js (code mode's tool on the
    # engine) or a bash call. Both start at once.
    atomic action ReplyWithAskAndRunJS:
        require self.status == "running" and self.asker == "none"
        self.asker = "pending"
        self.sibling = "running"
        self.tool = "js"

    atomic action ReplyWithAskAndBash:
        require self.status == "running" and self.asker == "none"
        self.asker = "pending"
        self.sibling = "running"
        self.tool = "bash"

    # The Asker puts the question in (oneAtATime, then putIn): the "ask"
    # entry is recorded and emitted; serve arms it, hlPrint sets hlAsk.
    atomic fair action AskOpens:
        require self.asker == "pending"
        self.asker = "open"
        self.armed = True
        self.hl = True
        self.ask = True
        self.status = "needs-you"

    # Engine.SiblingEnd. The sibling can end before or after the ask is
    # open. run_js records a "result" (plus a live "error" when it
    # failed); bash records a "call" with tool bash. None of these is
    # the ask's end, so nothing about the ask changes.
    # Product (B1/B2): if self.tool == "js": self.lose_ask(True, True, True)
    atomic fair action SiblingEndOk:
        require self.sibling == "running"
        self.sibling = "ok"

    atomic action SiblingEndFail:
        require self.sibling == "running"
        self.sibling = "failed"

    # Provider.FailsAfterSiblingEnd: the harness asked the model again
    # with the sibling's result, and the request failed. The error is
    # recorded; the Blocking ask keeps the turn open (as evaluate() does
    # for a blocking call) and stays answerable; the answer goes to the
    # model with the next request.
    # Product (B4): self.asker = "job"; self.lose_ask(True, True, True);
    #               self.status = "error"
    atomic action ProviderFails:
        require self.asker == "open" and self.sibling in ("ok", "failed") and not self.perr
        self.perr = True

    # System.StderrLine: the child writes a line on stderr; serve emits
    # it as "error" (live, never in history). Not the ask's end.
    # Product (B3): self.armed = False
    atomic action StderrLine:
        require self.asker == "open"
        pass

    # System.HookErrorNote: a recorded "error" entry mid-turn (a hook
    # blocking a steer, a refusal note). Not the ask's end.
    # Product (B3): self.armed = False; self.hl = False
    atomic action HookErrorNote:
        require self.asker == "open"
        pass

    # User.Answer: the page's buttons/composer while the row is
    # needs-you. POST /answer: 409 unless serve is armed; the line then
    # reaches hlAsk, and the Asker's call ends answered.
    atomic fair action Answer:
        require self.status == "needs-you"
        if self.armed and self.hl and self.asker == "open":
            self.asker = "answered"
            self.lose_ask(True, True, True)
            self.status = "running"

    # User.SendMessage: the page sends a message only while no question
    # is on screen (ask_answer's Send). /prompt is 409 while serve is
    # armed; otherwise the line reaches the child, where hlAsk decides.
    atomic action SendMessage:
        require not self.sent and self.status != "needs-you"
        self.sent = True
        if self.armed:
            pass
        elif self.hl and self.asker in ("open", "job"):
            self.msgAnswered = True
            self.asker = "answered"
            self.lose_ask(True, True, True)
            self.status = "running"
        else:
            if self.asker in ("open", "job"):
                self.steeredPast = True
            # A steer while running; a new turn otherwise.
            self.status = "running"

    # Asker.Timeout: 10 minutes pass with no answer. The call ends with
    # an error; its recorded end clears whatever still names the ask.
    # Not fair: the person is expected to answer first.
    atomic action Timeout:
        require self.asker in ("open", "job")
        if not (self.armed and self.hl and self.ask and self.asker == "open"):
            self.lost = True
        self.asker = "timedout"
        self.lose_ask(True, True, True)
        if self.status == "needs-you":
            self.status = "running"

    # User.Stop: the turn is cancelled; the ask's call and the sibling
    # end cancelled, and "cancelled" clears every arm.
    atomic action Stop:
        require self.status in ("running", "needs-you")
        if self.asker in ("pending", "open"):
            self.asker = "cancelled"
        if self.sibling == "running":
            self.sibling = "cancelled"
        self.lose_ask(True, True, True)
        self.status = "stopped"

    # The turn ends once nothing holds it: a reply was made, the ask is
    # resolved and the sibling has ended.
    atomic fair action Finish:
        require self.status == "running" and self.asker not in ("none", "pending", "open") and self.sibling != "running"
        self.lose_ask(True, True, True)
        if self.perr:
            self.status = "error"
        else:
            self.status = "done"

    atomic func lose_ask(arm, head, row):
        if arm:
            self.armed = False
        if head:
            self.hl = False
        if row:
            self.ask = False

action Init:
    session = Session()

# While the Asker has an open ask, serve's arm, headless hlAsk and the
# row's ask all name it; with no ask open, none of them does.
always assertion ArmsNameTheOpenAsk:
    o = session.asker == "open"
    return session.armed == o and session.hl == o and session.ask == o

# Another call's result or error, a stderr line or an error note never
# takes serve's arm off an open ask: the page's answer would 409.
always assertion ForeignEventNeverDisarms:
    return session.asker != "open" or session.armed

# A Blocking ask is never adopted as a job by providerError.
always assertion BlockingAskNeverAJob:
    return session.asker != "job"

# needs-you iff an ask is open (the child is alive throughout).
always assertion NeedsYouIffAskOpen:
    return (session.status == "needs-you") == (session.asker == "open")

# A message sent while an ask is open never steers past it, and is
# never taken as its answer.
always assertion MessageNeverSteersPastAsk:
    return not session.steeredPast and not session.msgAnswered

# An ask never runs out its 10 minutes unanswerable from the screen.
always assertion NoLostAsk:
    return not session.lost

# An open ask is eventually answered from the screen (the person
# answering is fair; the timeout is not).
always eventually assertion AskEventuallyResolved:
    return session.asker != "open" and session.asker != "pending"
